<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Orléans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1428">1428</a>–<a href="/years/1429">1429</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> was the English siege of the city of Orléans on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> from October 1428 to May 1429, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. Its fall would have opened the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to the English and their Burgundian allies, but at the end of April 1429 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> arrived with a relief force, and within days the French had taken the English forts and forced the besiegers to withdraw on 8 May. The victory turned the war in France's favour and led to the coronation of Charles VII at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> that July.</p></div>
